# Editorial debates: two opening cases each, written as their best defenders would put them.
# All positions below are held by faithful Christians. Verses are given as references (read from the KJV at display time).
def ED(title, cat, question, summary, aff, opp, aff_sum, opp_sum, a_body, a_verses, a_refs, o_body, o_verses, o_refs):
    return dict(title=title, cat=cat, question=question, summary=summary, aff=aff, opp=opp, aff_sum=aff_sum, opp_sum=opp_sum,
                entries=[("aff", "opening", "editors", a_body, a_verses, a_refs), ("opp", "opening", "editors", o_body, o_verses, o_refs)])

DEBATES += [
 ED("Who should be baptized: believers only, or believers and their children?", "theology",
  "Is baptism the sign of a person's own profession of faith, or the sign of God's covenant promise that also covers the children of believers?",
  "Christians agree that baptism is commanded by Christ and joined to the gospel. They differ on who should receive it and why. Both views are held by serious, Scripture-bound churches.",
  "Believers only (credobaptism)", "Believers and their children (paedobaptism)",
  "Baptism follows personal repentance and faith, and pictures the believer's union with Christ in his death and resurrection.",
  "Baptism is the sign of the covenant of grace, given to believers and to their children, in continuity with circumcision.",
  "The pattern of the New Testament is faith first, then baptism. The Great Commission says to make disciples and then baptize them (\"Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them...\"). At Pentecost Peter says \"Repent, and be baptized every one of you\", and the people who \"gladly received his word were baptized.\" When the Ethiopian asks what hinders his baptism, Philip answers: \"If thou believest with all thine heart, thou mayest.\" Paul explains baptism as the believer's burial and rising with Christ, which presupposes a person who has died to sin in conscious faith. The new covenant is also described as one in which all its members know the Lord, from the least to the greatest. For these reasons the sign should be given to those who can profess what it signifies.",
  ["Matthew 28:19-20", "Acts 2:38-41", "Acts 8:36-38", "Romans 6:3-4", "Jeremiah 31:34"],
  ["Beasley-Murray, G. (1962). Baptism in the New Testament. Eerdmans", "Schreiner, T. & Wright, S. (eds.) (2006). Believer's Baptism: Sign of the New Covenant in Christ. B&H Academic", "Jewett, P. (1978). Infant Baptism and the Covenant of Grace. Eerdmans"],
  "God has always dealt with his people as families. He told Abraham, \"I will establish my covenant between me and thee and thy seed after thee\", and commanded that the sign be given to infants. Paul calls baptism the circumcision \"made without hands\", which links the two signs directly. On the day of Pentecost Peter says \"the promise is unto you, and to your children\", and the New Testament records whole households baptized, among them Lydia's and the Philippian jailer's. Paul also calls the child of even one believing parent \"holy\". Nothing in the New Testament withdraws the inclusion of children. The sign shows what God promises, and the child is called to grow into faith. The early church baptized infants widely by the third century, and Cyprian treats it as settled.",
  ["Genesis 17:7-12", "Acts 2:39", "Acts 16:14-15", "Acts 16:31-33", "Colossians 2:11-12", "1 Corinthians 7:14"],
  ["Calvin, J. Institutes of the Christian Religion IV.16", "Westminster Confession of Faith, ch. 28", "Jeremias, J. (1960). Infant Baptism in the First Four Centuries. SCM", "Cyprian of Carthage, Epistle 64 (c. 253)"]),
 ED("How should Christians read Genesis 1: as a six-day history or as a literary account?", "biblical-interpretation",
  "Is Genesis 1 best understood as a straightforward chronological account of six ordinary days, or as an ordered, literary presentation of God as Creator?",
  "Faithful Christians who affirm that God created everything, that Adam and Eve were real, and that Scripture is true differ on how Genesis 1 should be read. The question concerns genre and interpretation, not whether God is Creator.",
  "A historical six-day account", "A literary-theological account",
  "Genesis 1 is historical narrative describing six ordinary days, and the plain reading is the most faithful one.",
  "Genesis 1 is carefully structured literature whose purpose is to show God as Creator and King, and its form guides how to read it.",
  "Genesis 1 is written as historical narrative: it uses the verb forms and rhythm of narrative, and each day closes with \"the evening and the morning\", the normal way of marking a day. The fourth commandment grounds the week in creation: \"in six days the LORD made heaven and earth.\" Jesus speaks of \"the beginning of the creation\" when he refers to the creation of male and female, treating them as real people. Paul builds his gospel on a historical Adam, through whom \"sin entered into the world, and death by sin.\" If Genesis 1-3 is not history, defenders ask, the link between the fall, death and Christ's redemptive work becomes harder to state. Believers can trust that God's word means what it plainly says.",
  ["Genesis 1:5", "Exodus 20:8-11", "Mark 10:6", "Romans 5:12-14", "1 Corinthians 15:21-22"],
  ["Mortenson, T. & Ury, T. (eds.) (2008). Coming to Grips with Genesis. Master Books", "Young, E. J. (1964). Studies in Genesis One. Presbyterian & Reformed", "Kulikovsky, A. (2009). Creation, Fall, Restoration. Mentor"],
  "Genesis 1 is a carefully patterned text. Days one to three form realms (light, sky and sea, land) and days four to six fill them with rulers and inhabitants, which suggests an artistic arrangement and not a diary. Ancient Near Eastern creation accounts describe gods creating temples to rest in, and Genesis answers with one God, no rivals, and a creation that is \"very good\". Scripture elsewhere speaks in poetic ways about creation, as in Psalm 104 and Job 38. Augustine, writing in the fifth century, already struggled with the \"days\" and thought God's act was not a matter of ordinary time. Reading Genesis 1 as theology does not weaken its authority. It reads the text for what its author meant, and leaves room for the study of nature, which Psalm 19 describes as telling of God's glory.",
  ["Genesis 1:1-2", "Genesis 2:4-5", "Psalm 19:1-4", "Psalm 104:1-9", "Job 38:4-11"],
  ["Walton, J. (2009). The Lost World of Genesis One. IVP Academic", "Blocher, H. (1984). In the Beginning. IVP", "Kline, M. (1996). 'Space and Time in the Genesis Cosmogony', Perspectives on Science and Christian Faith 48", "Augustine, The Literal Meaning of Genesis (c. 401-415)"]),
 ED("Is a person justified by faith alone?", "theology",
  "How is a sinner made right with God: through faith alone, with works as its fruit, or through a living faith that is formed and completed by love?",
  "This question divided the Reformation, and it still marks the difference between Protestant, Catholic and Orthodox teaching. In 1999 Lutherans and Catholics issued a joint declaration that they share a common understanding of the basic truths of justification, while some differences remain.",
  "Faith alone", "Faith working through love",
  "God declares sinners righteous on the basis of Christ's work alone, received through faith alone, and good works follow as the fruit of that faith.",
  "Justification is God's gift received by a faith that is alive in love and obedience, and the believer is truly made righteous by grace.",
  "Paul states it plainly: \"a man is justified by faith without the deeds of the law.\" Abraham \"believed God, and it was counted unto him for righteousness\", and Paul stresses that to one who does not work but believes, \"his faith is counted for righteousness.\" Salvation is \"by grace... through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: not of works, lest any man should boast.\" If anything of ours is added as a ground, assurance disappears and Christ's work is no longer enough. The Reformers did not teach that faith is idle. James, they said, describes the proof that faith is genuine, and Paul himself calls it \"faith which worketh by love.\" Good works follow from justification and are never its ground.",
  ["Romans 3:28", "Romans 4:2-5", "Galatians 2:16", "Ephesians 2:8-10", "Titus 3:5-7"],
  ["Luther, M. (1522). Preface to the Epistle to the Romans", "Westminster Confession of Faith, ch. 11", "Schreiner, T. (2015). Faith Alone: The Doctrine of Justification. Zondervan", "Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification (1999)"],
  "Scripture says that \"by grace are ye saved through faith\", and the Church agrees that we cannot earn salvation. But the faith that saves is not faith alone: \"in Christ Jesus neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision; but faith which worketh by love.\" James says \"by works a man is justified, and not by faith only\", and Jesus describes the judgment in terms of how people treated the least of his brothers. Paul warns that faith without love is nothing, even a faith that moves mountains. Justification is therefore not only a legal declaration, it is a real renewal by the grace of God, who works in us \"both to will and to do of his good pleasure.\" Catholic teaching and the Orthodox understanding of salvation as union with God both hold that grace is wholly God's gift and that it truly transforms the person.",
  ["Galatians 5:6", "James 2:21-24", "Matthew 25:31-46", "1 Corinthians 13:1-3", "Philippians 2:12-13"],
  ["Council of Trent (1547). Decree on Justification, Session 6", "Catechism of the Catholic Church, nos. 1987-2029", "Ware, K. (1979). The Orthodox Way. St Vladimir's Seminary Press", "Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification (1999)"]),
 ED("What is the final fate of the unrepentant: eternal torment or final destruction?", "theology",
  "Does Scripture teach that the lost will be consciously punished forever, or that they will finally be destroyed?",
  "Christians agree that there is a final judgment, that Christ is the only way of salvation, and that the justice of God will be vindicated. They differ on the nature of the punishment. Both views are defended by evangelical scholars.",
  "Eternal conscious punishment", "Conditional immortality",
  "The unrepentant will be punished consciously and without end, as the plain sense of Jesus' and John's words indicates.",
  "Immortality is a gift God gives in Christ, and the unrepentant will finally perish in a real and irreversible destruction.",
  "Jesus teaches that the wicked \"shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.\" The same word, \"everlasting\", is used for both, so the punishment is as long as the life. He speaks of a place \"where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched\", and of the rich man who is \"in torments\" and cannot cross over. Revelation says that \"the smoke of their torment ascendeth up for ever and ever.\" This has been the majority position of the church from Augustine through the Reformers and is held in the great creeds and confessions. It also takes seriously the weight of sin against an infinitely holy God. Defenders state it with sorrow, not with relish, and note that these warnings come mostly from the lips of Jesus himself.",
  ["Matthew 25:41-46", "Mark 9:43-48", "Luke 16:22-26", "Revelation 14:9-11"],
  ["Peterson, R. (1995). Hell on Trial. Presbyterian & Reformed", "Morgan, C. & Peterson, R. (eds.) (2004). Hell Under Fire. Zondervan", "Augustine, City of God XXI"],
  "Scripture describes the end of the wicked again and again as death and destruction. \"The wages of sin is death\", and \"whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life.\" Jesus warns us to fear him who \"is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.\" Paul says the lost will be \"punished with everlasting destruction\", and Paul says that God alone \"hath immortality\", so no one is naturally deathless. The idea that every soul is by nature immortal came into the church from Greek philosophy more than from the Bible. The punishment is everlasting because its result is: an irreversible end. John Stott said he held this view with hesitation, but thought it deserved evangelical consideration, and the 2000 report of the Evangelical Alliance said that both views are within evangelical faith.",
  ["Romans 6:23", "John 3:16", "Matthew 10:28", "2 Thessalonians 1:8-9", "1 Timothy 6:15-16"],
  ["Fudge, E. (1982; 3rd ed. 2011). The Fire That Consumes. Cascade", "Stott, J. & Edwards, D. (1988). Evangelical Essentials. IVP", "Evangelical Alliance Commission on Unity and Truth among Evangelicals (2000). The Nature of Hell"]),
 ED("Do the miraculous spiritual gifts continue today?", "theology",
  "Are gifts such as prophecy, tongues and healing still given to the church, or were they signs of the apostolic age that have ceased?",
  "Christians across the world agree that the Holy Spirit is active and that prayer for the sick is right. They differ about whether the miraculous gifts of the New Testament continue in the same form.",
  "The gifts continue (continuationism)", "The gifts ceased (cessationism)",
  "Nothing in Scripture says the gifts end before Christ returns, and the church has reported them throughout its history.",
  "The miraculous gifts authenticated the apostles and the founding revelation, and they ended when that foundation was complete.",
  "Paul writes to the Corinthians about gifts that he expects the church to \"covet\" and to use in order, and he says they will continue until \"that which is perfect is come\", which most naturally means the return of Christ, when we see \"face to face.\" Peter at Pentecost quotes Joel that in \"the last days\" God would pour out his Spirit and people would prophesy, and the last days continue. Paul tells the Thessalonians not to \"despise prophesyings\" but to \"prove all things\", which assumes that prophecy would continue and need testing. Christians in many places today report healings and answered prayer, and the historian Craig Keener has collected extensive accounts. Continuationists urge order, humility and the weighing of every claim against Scripture.",
  ["1 Corinthians 12:4-11", "1 Corinthians 13:8-12", "Acts 2:17-18", "1 Thessalonians 5:19-21"],
  ["Grudem, W. (1994). Systematic Theology, ch. 30-31. Zondervan", "Keener, C. (2011). Miracles: The Credibility of the New Testament Accounts. Baker", "Storms, S. (2017). Practicing the Power. Zondervan"],
  "Miracles in Scripture cluster around moments of revelation, such as Moses, Elijah and Christ with his apostles. Hebrews says that salvation was \"confirmed unto us by them that heard him, God also bearing them witness, both with signs and wonders\", and Paul calls \"signs, and wonders, and mighty deeds\" the \"signs of an apostle.\" The church is \"built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets\", a foundation laid once. B. B. Warfield argued that the gifts served that foundation and passed with it, and Richard Gaffin has made a careful case from Paul's own words that \"when that which is perfect is come\" is better read as the completion of revelation than of the Lord's return. None of this denies that God answers prayer and heals today. It holds that he does so in his own sovereignty, and not by apostolic gifts given to the church.",
  ["Hebrews 2:3-4", "2 Corinthians 12:12", "Ephesians 2:19-20", "1 Corinthians 13:8-10"],
  ["Warfield, B. B. (1918). Counterfeit Miracles. Scribner", "Gaffin, R. (1979). Perspectives on Pentecost. Presbyterian & Reformed", "Edgar, T. (1988). Miracles and Healing. Zondervan"]),
 ED("Does the authority of the Bible require its inerrancy?", "biblical-interpretation",
  "Must Christians affirm that Scripture is without error in everything it asserts, or can its full authority be held with a more qualified account of its truthfulness?",
  "Christians agree that the Bible is God's word and the church's supreme authority. They differ on how to describe its truthfulness and on how to account for features such as differences between parallel accounts.",
  "Yes: Scripture is inerrant", "Authority without technical inerrancy",
  "Because God cannot lie and Scripture is God-breathed, it is true and without error in all that it affirms.",
  "Scripture is fully authoritative and trustworthy for its purposes, and its human character should be taken as seriously as its divine origin.",
  "Paul says that \"all scripture is given by inspiration of God.\" Jesus says that \"the scripture cannot be broken\" and that not \"one jot or one tittle\" will pass from the law. Scripture also says that God \"cannot lie\" and \"is not a man, that he should lie.\" If the words are God's, and God does not err, it follows that Scripture does not err in what it teaches. The 1978 Chicago Statement defines the term carefully: it allows for ordinary language, round numbers, and different literary forms, and asks only that the text be true in what it affirms. Defenders hold that when the Bible's reliability is lost at one point, believers lose their footing on others, since Jesus himself relied on the details of the Old Testament.",
  ["2 Timothy 3:16-17", "John 10:34-35", "Matthew 5:17-18", "Titus 1:1-2", "Numbers 23:19"],
  ["Chicago Statement on Biblical Inerrancy (1978)", "Warfield, B. B. (1948). The Inspiration and Authority of the Bible. Presbyterian & Reformed", "Beale, G. K. (2008). The Erosion of Inerrancy in Evangelicalism. Crossway"],
  "The Bible's own claims are that it is \"profitable\" for teaching and for making us wise unto salvation, and Luke tells us he wrote after careful investigation so that the reader \"mightest know the certainty.\" Those who hold this view affirm that Scripture is the infallible rule of faith and life and that it never fails in what it intends to teach. The question is whether \"inerrancy\" in its technical modern sense is the right word. The four Gospels tell the same events with differences of order and wording, and the New Testament cites the Old with freedom. Peter Enns argues that these features show that God spoke through real human authors in real settings, in the pattern of the incarnation. The Reformers confessed Scripture's authority without that word, and Karl Barth described it as a witness through which Christ speaks. This position holds that full trust in Scripture does not depend on a theory of its precision.",
  ["2 Timothy 3:15-17", "Luke 1:1-4", "1 Corinthians 7:10-12", "Hebrews 1:1-2"],
  ["Enns, P. (2005; 2nd ed. 2015). Inspiration and Incarnation. Baker Academic", "Barth, K. Church Dogmatics I/2, section 19", "Vanhoozer, K. (2016). Biblical Authority after Babel. Brazos"]),
 ED("Why does God permit evil: the free-will defense or soul-making?", "philosophy",
  "Which Christian account best explains why a good and almighty God permits suffering: the value of free will, or the growth of persons through hardship?",
  "Every Christian tradition has to speak about evil. These two classic answers are often combined, and their defenders differ about which carries the main weight.",
  "The free-will defense", "Soul-making (the Irenaean view)",
  "God made creatures with real freedom, and the possibility of moral evil is the cost of a world in which love and obedience are real.",
  "God is bringing persons from immaturity toward maturity in him, and hardship and risk are part of that process.",
  "Love cannot be coerced. God made humans able to choose him, and the Bible traces the entry of evil to a real choice: \"by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin.\" Moses sets before Israel \"life and death, blessing and cursing: therefore choose life.\" James denies that God tempts anyone, and says each person is drawn away by his own desire. Augustine, and in our time Alvin Plantinga, have argued that it is not logically impossible for God to have a good reason for allowing evil, since a world with free creatures who freely do right is better than a world of puppets. Natural evil is traced to the fall of creation and to spiritual powers opposed to God. Christians add that God has not stayed distant from the evil he permits: he has entered it, suffered it and promised to end it.",
  ["Genesis 3:1-7", "Deuteronomy 30:19", "Romans 5:12", "James 1:13-15", "Romans 8:19-23"],
  ["Plantinga, A. (1974). God, Freedom, and Evil. Eerdmans", "Augustine, On Free Choice of the Will; Enchiridion", "van Inwagen, P. (2006). The Problem of Evil. Oxford University Press"],
  "Irenaeus taught that humans were created immature, like children, and meant to grow into the likeness of God. Scripture says that even Christ \"learned obedience by the things which he suffered\", and that tribulation produces \"patience; and patience, experience; and experience, hope.\" James tells believers to \"count it all joy\" when they face trials because they work toward being \"perfect and entire.\" Paul says that present sufferings are not worth comparing with the glory to come. John Hick developed this into a theodicy in which a world with real challenges is the setting where persons can become, freely, children of God. Eleonore Stump, drawing on Aquinas, argues that God's aim is union with each person and that suffering can serve that end. Job's answer teaches humility about our limited view. These writers do not say that evil is good, but that God can bring good out of it, and that he does not leave anyone to face it alone.",
  ["Hebrews 5:8-9", "Romans 5:3-5", "James 1:2-4", "2 Corinthians 4:16-18", "Job 38:1-4"],
  ["Hick, J. (1966). Evil and the God of Love. Macmillan", "Irenaeus, Against Heresies IV.38", "Stump, E. (2010). Wandering in Darkness. Oxford University Press"]),
 ED("Can philosophy and Christianity be reconciled?", "philosophy",
  "Should Christians draw on the tradition of philosophy, or does it rest on assumptions that conflict with the gospel?",
  "From Tertullian's question, 'What has Athens to do with Jerusalem?', to Aquinas's claim that grace perfects nature, Christians have taken positions ranging from suspicion to full engagement. Both views are held by serious believers.",
  "Reconcilable", "Fundamentally in tension",
  "Reason is a gift of God, and philosophy can serve faith by clarifying, defending and applying it, provided its assumptions are tested.",
  "The wisdom of the world and the wisdom of the cross differ in kind, so the church should be wary of letting philosophy set the terms of theology.",
  "Paul at the Areopagus quotes Greek poets and builds his message on what the Athenians already knew, and Luke says he 'reasoned' in the synagogue and the marketplace. Isaiah invites, 'Come now, and let us reason together', and Paul says God's invisible qualities are 'clearly seen' in what has been made. Justin Martyr thought the philosophers had grasped part of the Word, and Augustine compared borrowed learning to the gold the Israelites took from Egypt: it belongs to God and can be put to better use. Aquinas built his theology in dialogue with Aristotle, and John Paul II described faith and reason as two wings of the human spirit. The task is discernment, not avoidance, and Paul's own counsel is to 'prove all things; hold fast that which is good.'",
  ["Acts 17:2", "Acts 17:22-31", "Isaiah 1:18", "Romans 1:19-20", "1 Thessalonians 5:21-22"],
  ["Augustine, On Christian Doctrine II.40", "Justin Martyr, Second Apology 13", "Aquinas, T. Summa Contra Gentiles I.1-9", "John Paul II (1998). Fides et Ratio"],
  "Tertullian asked, 'What has Athens to do with Jerusalem?' and warned that heresies are 'instigated by philosophy.' Paul says that 'the world by wisdom knew not God', that God chose 'the foolish things of the world to confound the wise', and warns, 'Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men.' The gospel is not the conclusion of an argument. It is news about something done in history. Luther contrasted the theologian of glory, who climbs to God by reason, with the theologian of the cross, who finds God hidden in weakness, and Karl Barth rejected natural theology for the same reason. The history of borrowing gives cause for care: Greek thought about the soul's natural immortality entered the church, though the New Testament hope is resurrection. Philosophy can serve as a tool, but when it sets the terms, it reshapes the message.",
  ["1 Corinthians 1:18-25", "Colossians 2:8", "1 Corinthians 2:1-5", "Romans 1:21-22"],
  ["Tertullian, Prescription Against Heretics 7 (c. 200)", "Luther, M. (1518). Heidelberg Disputation, theses 19-21", "Barth, K. (1934). Nein! Antwort an Emil Brunner", "Cullmann, O. (1958). Immortality of the Soul or Resurrection of the Dead?"]),
 ED("Should the virtuous person aim to be free of passion?", "philosophy",
  "Is the ideal of the good person a calm freedom from the passions (apatheia), or the right ordering of emotions such as grief, anger and love?",
  "The Stoics taught that wisdom frees us from disturbing passions. Christian thinkers have borrowed from them at times and criticized them at others. Both views have defenders among those who take Scripture seriously.",
  "Freedom from passion (Stoic-influenced)", "Rightly ordered passions",
  "Peace comes from mastering the passions through right judgment and trust, and contentment in every circumstance is a mark of maturity.",
  "Emotions are part of how a good person responds to a fallen world, so the goal is love that is rightly ordered, not a heart that feels nothing.",
  "Paul says, 'I have learned, in whatsoever state I am, therewith to be content', and that he knows how to be abased and how to abound. Jesus tells his disciples not to be anxious about tomorrow and promises a peace 'not as the world giveth.' The Stoics held that distress comes from false judgments about what matters, and Christian writers from Clement of Alexandria to Evagrius admired apatheia, a calm no loss can overturn, though they rooted it in God and not in self-mastery. On this view the believer is steady because his treasure is secure: 'set your affection on things above.' Passions that overturn judgment, such as rage, envy and panic, are the enemies of wisdom, and training against them is part of sanctification.",
  ["Philippians 4:11-13", "Matthew 6:31-34", "John 14:27", "Colossians 3:1-2"],
  ["Epictetus, Enchiridion 1-5 (c. 125)", "Clement of Alexandria, Stromateis VI-VII", "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos (4th c.)", "Seneca, Letters to Lucilius"],
  "Jesus wept at the tomb of Lazarus, and in Gethsemane he said, 'My soul is exceeding sorrowful, even unto death.' Paul commands, 'Rejoice with them that do rejoice, and weep with them that weep.' Augustine takes up the Stoic ideal directly in the City of God: Christians feel fear, grief and desire, but these are right when they follow the love of God and neighbor, and to feel nothing in a world of sin would be hardness of heart, not virtue. Aquinas sums it up: the passions are not evil in themselves but become good when ordered by reason and charity. Martha Nussbaum argues, in a different key, that emotions are judgments about what matters, and that removing them denies that anything matters. A God who is love is not best imitated by indifference.",
  ["John 11:33-35", "Matthew 26:37-38", "Romans 12:15", "Hebrews 4:15"],
  ["Augustine, City of God IX.4-5; XIV.8-9", "Aquinas, T. Summa Theologiae I-II, qq. 22-24", "Nussbaum, M. (2001). Upheavals of Thought. Cambridge University Press"]),
 ED("Can Christians practice philosophy as a way of life?", "faith-reason",
  "Can Christians adopt the spiritual exercises of the ancient philosophers (attention, daily self-examination, imagining loss), or do they carry a view of life that conflicts with the gospel?",
  "Pierre Hadot argued that ancient philosophy was a way of life with daily exercises, not only a body of theory. Christians have long borrowed from these (Basil, Augustine, the monastic tradition) and have also warned against them.",
  "Compatible, with discernment", "Cautions against adopting them",
  "Disciplines of attention and self-examination are good tools that Christian practice has adopted before, as long as they serve the worship of God.",
  "These practices come with a goal, self-sufficiency, that differs from the gospel's dependence on grace, so believers should prefer practices drawn from Scripture and the church.",
  "Christian monasticism took over the shape of ancient philosophical practice: the daily examination of conscience practiced by Seneca became the examen, and attention to the present moment became watchfulness. Paul tells the Philippians to think on whatever is true, honest, just and pure, and the psalmist prays, 'So teach us to number our days, that we may apply our hearts unto wisdom.' Justin Martyr counted himself a philosopher after becoming a Christian, and Hadot's work shows that many early believers understood Christianity as the true philosophy. Christians already use tools from many sources. The sensible path is to use the exercise, aim it at God, and keep the content of faith distinct from the method.",
  ["Philippians 4:8", "Psalm 90:12", "1 Thessalonians 5:21-22", "Psalm 139:23-24"],
  ["Hadot, P. (1995). Philosophy as a Way of Life. Blackwell", "Basil of Caesarea, Homily on the words 'Give heed to thyself'", "Justin Martyr, Dialogue with Trypho 8"],
  "Hadot himself notes that the Stoic exercise aims at self-sufficiency: the sage depends on his own judgment, and nothing outside him can harm him. That differs in kind from the gospel, where 'by grace are ye saved... not of yourselves' and where the believer's strength is 'made perfect in weakness.' Paul warns, 'Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit.' Practices shape their practitioners, so a technique designed for self-mastery can quietly teach self-reliance. Tertullian worried about a 'Stoic and Platonic Christianity', and Luther distinguished the theologian of the cross, who finds God in weakness, from the theologian of glory, who climbs by his own effort. Believers can pursue attention and self-examination through prayer, the Psalms and the church's own disciplines, which are shaped by the aim of knowing Christ.",
  ["Colossians 2:8", "Ephesians 2:8-9", "2 Corinthians 12:9-10", "Psalm 119:15-16"],
  ["Hadot, P. (1995), cited above", "Tertullian, Prescription Against Heretics 7", "Luther, M. (1518). Heidelberg Disputation, theses 19-21"]),
]
